{"kind":"request","text":"codex: a chess repository created before yesterday evening cannot be opened by the current chess binary, and our documentation tells its owner that is unrecoverable when the log already supports recovery. Hugh hit this today.\n\nTHE ERROR. \"repository is verifiable but uninterpretable: it is bound to chess fold \"chess-fold@0\", and this build holds \"chess-fold@2\"\". That is internal/app/host.go comparing held.foldVersion against the recorded binding by exact string equality, and the refusal itself is correct: a build that does not hold a fold should not pretend to interpret records written under it.\n\nWHAT I TRACED. In the chess repository, chess.go carried chess-fold@0 at d9a1ed7 on 2026-08-25. It became chess-fold@1 at 54fbaa8 on 2026-08-26 at 20:56, the first-user review-blocker repair, and chess-fold@2 at 0aa8172 at 21:41, the atomic named-game creation. Both bumps sit inside the first-user lane that I coordinated and that merged this morning as 5c29123. So every chess game repository initialized before yesterday evening is now unopenable by the shipped binary, and that is a consequence of work I shepherded rather than something that drifted in on its own.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT SIMPLY A KNOWN COST. The mechanism already exists. apphost.BindingInForce scans the log in order and keeps the last qualifying binding record, and its own comment says the bootstrap binding and a later replacement are one rule rather than two. Authority is the initializing actor, and anything unauthorized or malformed is passed over precisely so that nobody able to append can make a repository unreadable. A replacement binding naming chess-fold@2 is exactly what recovers hugh's repository.\n\nBut host/host.go tells the reader the opposite — that the choice is permanent, and that recovery takes the application at the bound version rather than a repair. Someone who reads the error and then the documentation is sent to look for an old binary. And there is no command that writes a binding: gs runs from init to attach with nothing for it, so today the mechanism is reachable only by hand-writing the record.\n\nTHE PART I WANT STATED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Rebinding authorises a new fold to interpret old records; it does not prove the new fold judges them the same way. The version exists because judgements changed. So a bump anyone will migrate across owes evidence that the new fold reproduces the old judgements on the existing log — the shape internal/workroom/testdata/legacy_projection.golden.json already has — or an honest list of what differs and why that is acceptable. Without that, a rebind command is a way to silently reinterpret history, which is worse than the refusal it replaces.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. Not a weaker gate. The repair is a documented, authorised, evidenced way to move the binding.\n\nONE THING FOR CHESS, once the surface exists. Chess went from 0 to 2 in under an hour and nobody has said whether either bump changed a judgement on records already written. If both are conservative, migrating hugh's repository is mechanical. If either is not, that needs saying before anything reinterprets his games. I am not folding that into this request, but it is the first thing this work makes answerable.","body":{"conditions":"Three parts; one head, or propose a split. (1) Correct the documentation contradiction. host/host.go's package comment says a repository declares its application once and that the choice is permanent, and ErrUninterpretable says recovering the meaning takes the application at the bound version rather than a repair. internal/apphost.BindingInForce implements and documents the opposite: the bootstrap binding and a later replacement are one rule, scanned in order, keeping the last that qualifies. Make the host package say what the code does, including who may replace a binding (the initializing actor whose key signed the log's first record) and that an unauthorized, unparseable or malformed binding-shaped record is passed over rather than raised, leaving the previous answer standing. (2) Give the mechanism an operator surface: a way to record a replacement binding that refuses when the caller is not the initializing actor, names the genesis and the exact outgoing and incoming fold versions in what it writes, and refuses a binding identical to the one in force. CLI at least; MCP if it costs little. (3) State the soundness obligation in the reference documentation: replacing a binding authorises a new fold to interpret existing records but does not establish that it judges them the same way, so a fold-version bump that anyone will migrate across needs evidence that the new fold reproduces the old judgements on the existing log, in the manner of internal/workroom/testdata/legacy_projection.golden.json, or an enumeration of the differences and why they are acceptable. Tests, each written from the desired behaviour: a replacement recorded by the initializing actor changes what BindingInForce returns and lets a build at the new version open a repository bound to the old one; a replacement attempted by any other key leaves the previous binding in force; a malformed binding-shaped record leaves the previous binding in force. Do NOT change the exact-equality comparison in internal/app/host.go: refusing a fold version this build does not hold is correct, and loosening it to a range or a floor would trade a loud correct refusal for a quiet wrong interpretation. gofmt, build, vet and the full suite green at the exact head with -count=1; exact clean pushed head with per-path artifacts; independent review before merge. Write no initiative or type tags: proposal 5409bc64 is unratified.","severity":"high","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}